00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.810
but now I have to pay them
a lot of money for it.

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:00.260
But I'm happy to be here.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:04.480
We're a very happy Google
customer as I bet many of you

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:05.740
are.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:09.890
So mabl, we started that company
about a year and a half ago.

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:14.020
And we started basically with
a very broad sort of approach.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:16.930
And we interviewed
hundreds of software teams.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.420
And we would sit
down and ask them

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:22.460
basically, well, what's working
well, what's not working well.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:23.810
And we heard a bunch of things.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:26.020
So we heard that as
an industry, we're

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.300
moving toward
continuous integration,

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.350
continuous delivery, DevOps.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:32.590
Raise your hand if you
have a lot of work underway

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.900
to move to DevOps, CI/CD?

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:35.860
Yeah, all right.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:37.585
So we're all going there.

00:01:37.585 --> 00:01:39.460
Raise your hand if you
feel like you're done,

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:41.170
everything's going great.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:43.540
Hey, one, two, three, good.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:46.180
And so another
thing that we heard

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:50.500
was that there is an emerging
bottleneck in that journey.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:52.522
And that's around
software testing.

00:01:52.522 --> 00:01:53.980
And so the thesis
that we developed

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.290
is actually that
software testing

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:58.720
needs to evolve if
we as an industry

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.640
can achieve the potential
of DevOps and CI/CD.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.950
And so we set out to build mabl.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:10.780
And this quote
left a mark on me.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.250
One of the QA
leaders that we spent

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:15.160
a lot of time with very
early on said, hey Dan,

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.890
let me just put
it very directly.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:19.960
My teams are shipping
features at a pace

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.370
now that I can't keep up.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:24.460
There is just not
enough time to test.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:27.610
Does that sound familiar,
I'm sure, to some of you?

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.390
And so we figured
out-- we thought, well,

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:32.440
how can we help him solve this?

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:36.790
And what we believe is that
we've reached a tipping point,

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:38.950
as you start to approach
continuous integration,

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:41.440
continuous delivery,
where fundamentally

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.030
the ways that we've
thought about QA

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:46.330
historically don't
really apply anymore.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:49.240
I actually bumped into a former
colleague, Marius back there.

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:50.180
Where's Marius?

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:54.880
Marius, he and I were sitting
in a room probably 13 years ago

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.300
planning our annual release
at Microsoft, right?

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.840
And so we would talk about, OK,
how many months, how many weeks

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.970
do we need for QA
for this release?

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:06.880
And we'd work through a
detailed plan incorporating

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.940
all the changes that we expected
to happen to the product based

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:11.756
on a functional spec.

00:03:11.756 --> 00:03:13.880
A lot of people are in
functional specs these days?

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:15.850
Yeah, a little bit?

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:18.220
And now, we're at a point
in many environments,

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:19.810
or at least we want
to get to a place

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:22.720
where change is just continuous.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.990
So you can't plan for
a given change anymore.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:26.830
It's continuous.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:31.092
We're talking about an order
of magnitude more releases.

00:03:31.092 --> 00:03:32.800
And so the challenge
to us as an industry

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.890
is, how can we achieve that
velocity while still having

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:39.440
confidence that we're delivering
high-quality product to our end

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:40.310
users?

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:43.650
So this is an illustration
actually of our team at mabl.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:46.180
So we only have, I
don't know, a handful

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.160
of people contributing
to this project, which

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.260
is our UI project.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:52.750
And we're doing, I don't
know, as many as 100 commits

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:55.607
that get pushed to
production every single week.

00:03:55.607 --> 00:03:56.690
And this is a little team.

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:58.660
Now imagine where
orders of magnitude,

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:02.170
as I know many of your teams
are orders of magnitude larger.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:03.890
So think of that pace of change.

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:07.059
How can we make sure that we're
shipping high-quality product?

00:04:07.059 --> 00:04:08.350
And these aren't small changes.

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:11.680
We're talking about
thousands, tens of thousands

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:16.480
of lines of code, shipping to
production every single week.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.459
And this is to ignore
the fact that we

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:22.960
update dependencies, configs,
and so forth every single day.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:24.790
And that's all change
that could lead

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:28.210
to a broken product for users.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:30.730
And so the challenge
to us is, how do we

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:34.210
maintain this velocity while
achieving good confidence

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:36.550
that we can trust the product
that goes out the door

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:40.660
and maintain our users
trust based on that?

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:46.310
And to make the challenge
even greater as QA leaders,

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:50.050
as VPs of engineering,
we are being

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:51.880
tasked to make sure
that our products work

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:56.770
on more devices, more browsers,
more resolutions, more

00:04:56.770 --> 00:05:00.700
permutations of the client
experience than ever before.

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:03.220
And so now you think
about that QA leader

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:07.770
that has this pace of change
that's just unbelievable coming

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.780
into the team.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:11.670
And on top of that,
we're being tasked

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:13.290
to say, let's make
sure that it works

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:16.410
across a wide
variety of devices.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:20.010
And so some teams are struggling
to say, how do I build out

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:21.870
the infrastructure that
I need to make sure

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:24.750
that I can test effectively
across all the client

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:28.230
experiences that
I have to support?

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:32.070
And the risk is that
we end up in a position

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:38.220
where the QA function or process
is a bottleneck for development

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:39.080
teams.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:41.460
And so think about all
the innovation that's

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:44.580
happened over the last 10 years,
development tools and processes

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:47.875
that enable you as an engineer
to build and ship code faster,

00:05:47.875 --> 00:05:49.500
think of all the
great innovations that

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:51.210
have happened in the
DevOps space for you

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:53.989
as a sys admin or an
operator to release

00:05:53.989 --> 00:05:56.280
that code to production, and
monitor it, and manage it.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:00.000
Well, where's the
innovation for QA?

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.674
And so this is what
we're focused on.

00:06:01.674 --> 00:06:03.090
And we actually
believe that we're

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:06.090
at the beginning of
a new era of what

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:08.250
we call intelligent testing.

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:11.340
And really, this is
intelligent test automation.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:14.710
And there are some principles
that we believe in here.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:19.360
The first one is that tests have
to be able to adapt to change.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:21.510
And what that means is
as the product evolves,

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:26.070
it has to be very infrequent
that the tests blow up

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:29.160
because of a change to the
product that doesn't actually

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:31.330
affect the user experience.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:34.140
The second principle is that
we think that tests are going

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:36.150
to be running in the cloud.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:39.150
We'll talk in a minute
about how automated testing,

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.220
like the execution
of automated tests,

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:44.760
is the perfect use case
for cloud computing.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:50.220
Third, we'll talk about how
the tests that get executed,

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:52.800
the output, that we can't be
in the business of reviewing

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.110
the output of every
test execution

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:58.350
that goes by to confirm
that there aren't issues,

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:01.980
we need to apply analysis
to that output to tell us

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:03.700
when there are issues.

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:05.610
And that's a really
important shift.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:07.672
And then finally,
this all has to happen

00:07:07.672 --> 00:07:08.880
in the context of a pipeline.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:11.640
We can't have QA sort
of off to the side

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:16.020
trying to inspect releases as
they go through because again,

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:19.844
the pace is such that it has
to be automatic and automated.

00:07:19.844 --> 00:07:21.010
So those are the principles.

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:23.320
And what we'll do
now is we'll explore

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:25.600
each principle in
detail and show you

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:28.880
how it works when we
integrate with GCP.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:32.410
GCP actually provides a
really compelling foundation

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:35.600
for solving a lot
of these problems.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:37.790
And so the first
principle was tests

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:40.220
have to be able to
adapt to change.

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:44.120
If any of you have worked with
legacy testing frameworks,

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:45.890
you've probably
struggled a little bit

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:50.810
with tests that can survive
changes to your front end,

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:51.320
for example.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:54.200
Anybody work with
Selenium, Selenium?

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:55.280
Yeah, OK.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:57.320
And anybody built great
Selenium test suite

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:59.810
that can seamlessly
handle dramatic changes

00:07:59.810 --> 00:08:02.330
to the front end
without false positives?

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:03.560
Not one hand.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:05.220
Maybe an elbow back there.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:05.910
Yeah, almost.

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:08.360
Oh, down, that was thumbs down.

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:10.490
And so, one of the
problems we need to solve

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:13.940
is exactly this, to
make sure that tests

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:17.300
don't break when the user
experience isn't affected.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:19.250
Because when you
have that situation,

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:22.460
and you're trying to deploy
changes for production rapidly,

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:24.740
false positives and
tests can create

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:26.630
all sorts of dysfunction.

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:29.360
Either you stop the
release, creating a backup

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:32.659
in the pipeline, or you end
up with developers saying,

00:08:32.659 --> 00:08:34.970
hey QA, your tests,
those are bad tests.

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:36.961
And QA is saying, well,
you broke the test.

00:08:36.961 --> 00:08:37.960
Why don't you go fix it?

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:40.126
Anybody been in that-- we've
been in that situation.

00:08:40.126 --> 00:08:41.659
I don't even have to ask.

00:08:41.659 --> 00:08:45.890
And so, we created mabl
just as an illustration

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:47.700
to be intent-driven.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:52.670
And so what that means is
we use a Chrome extension.

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:58.010
And as a QA engineer, or a
product owner, or a support

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:02.420
person, or anyone else, you
basically use your application

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:04.340
as you would expect
end users to,

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:08.270
and then mabl captures
the intent of your actions

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:10.370
and generates tests
based on that.

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:12.740
And we'll talk about how
that works in just a second.

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:15.080
An important thing here is
to look at the area in red

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:18.350
where it says, "click
the Close Alert button."

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:22.940
And so that's just a step
that mabl recorded as you

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:26.360
were using the application.

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:30.510
Now, when we run that
test in the cloud,

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:33.150
it could be that when
we get to that step--

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:35.310
so you look at the area
that's highlighted in red--

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:40.320
that that button now says,
"Dismiss" and the XPath

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:40.820
changed.

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:44.360
The XPath is the location
of that button in the DOM,

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:45.840
the structure of the web page.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:49.880
Now historically, this would
be a disaster for your test

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:53.840
because anything that interacted
with that button would fail.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:56.030
What we've done with
mabl is implemented

00:09:56.030 --> 00:09:59.660
a set of heuristics that
say, OK, given the intent

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:03.830
to click that button,
let's go figure out

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:05.480
how to find the
button if we can't

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:08.970
find it using the locators
that we had originally.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:12.350
And so we've built a model of
that button and every element

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:13.800
that we interact with.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:16.460
And so, if we can't find the
button where it used to be

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:18.710
we'll find the button using
a different heuristic,

00:10:18.710 --> 00:10:21.560
and we'll make sure that
the test continues to pass.

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:24.470
And then if it does,
we'll update the test.

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:26.450
And so this is an
example of how you

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:29.330
can create tests
that can survive

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:31.970
these dramatic and
frequent changes

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:34.315
in at least the front
end of an application.

00:10:34.315 --> 00:10:35.690
And so Joe will
talk a little bit

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:37.730
about how we do that on GCP.

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:38.835
JOSEPH LUST: Thanks Dan.

00:10:38.835 --> 00:10:42.244
So as Dan was saying, and
as any developer here knows,

00:10:42.244 --> 00:10:43.910
the real problem with
continuous testing

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:45.440
these days is brittle tests.

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:48.140
You check in to commit,
6,000 tests fail.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:50.720
So we really do need to make
these tests automatically

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:51.319
adapt.

00:10:51.319 --> 00:10:53.360
And not only do we make
them automatically adapt,

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:55.940
the system has to adapt because
nobody can go through 6,000

00:10:55.940 --> 00:10:58.190
tests to fix that CSS bug.

00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:02.810
So here at mabl is how in GCP
we built a continuous, seamless

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:04.820
adaptation workflow.

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:07.490
We start with everything
in the bottom right

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:11.257
here, the Cloud Datastore,
which is our storage

00:11:11.257 --> 00:11:13.340
place for all the scripts
that we're going to run,

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:14.540
all the tests.

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:17.710
And then these tests get
loaded into our testing cluster

00:11:17.710 --> 00:11:19.700
in Kubernetes,
which is going to be

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:23.240
able to run hundreds of parallel
instances of that browser.

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:26.300
And they're all going to emit
tons of observations, hundreds

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:27.800
a second, as Dan
mentioned how we're

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:31.590
building that model of
that page, of that element.

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:34.010
And this continuous flow
goes through Cloud Pub/Sub

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:35.750
and into Cloud Dataflow.

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:37.520
We can do continuous
stream processing

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:39.590
and transforms on this data.

00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:42.380
And we can determine,
did something odd happen?

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:43.760
Did we not find the button?

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:45.530
Did it change its
text, but we think

00:11:45.530 --> 00:11:47.300
we know where it really is?

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:49.580
And in some cases, we
might not know where it is.

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:51.830
In that case, we would go
out to Cloud Functions.

00:11:51.830 --> 00:11:54.290
We can call a script,
send a Slack message,

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:56.910
let our user know that
something has gone wrong.

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:58.820
But in most cases,
we can actually

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:00.500
start closing this loop.

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:04.100
We can figure out that Dismiss
is actually the correct button.

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:06.440
And we can automatically
change that script and say,

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:08.930
this is the button
you need to click.

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:11.000
And we can feed back
into our data store,

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:13.817
we can update that script,
and the next time we run,

00:12:13.817 --> 00:12:16.400
we know exactly where it is, and
we don't have to adapt again.

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:18.500
The test has been healed.

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:21.080
And by doing this,
this continuous flow,

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:25.440
we seamlessly adapt and are able
to achieve continuous testing.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:29.850
DAN BELCHER: Great,
thank you very much, Joe.

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:33.740
And so that's how-- that's
a general pattern for how

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:38.430
we can create tests that
survive despite changes

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:40.200
to the structure of
the underlying page.

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:42.150
So practically speaking,
what this means

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:45.690
is that for our users,
putting a maintenance message

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:47.280
at the top of the
page that changes

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:49.890
the DOM and the XPaths of
all the elements on the page

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:51.480
doesn't break the tests.

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:54.210
Changing the IDs of elements
doesn't break the test.

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:57.300
Changing the CSS classes
don't break the test.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:59.250
And so that makes
it so that you don't

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:03.390
have a lot of noise around sort
of maintaining the test as part

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:06.060
of the pipeline.

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:07.310
So that's the first principle.

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:09.920
Second principle, tests
will run in the cloud.

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:12.560
We predict that
three years from now,

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:13.770
we're sitting here talking.

00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:16.730
And if I ask how many
people are building out

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:20.060
big farms of test
infrastructure for on-premise,

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:22.740
we think very few hands
are going to go up.

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:24.960
And really, that's because,
as I mentioned before,

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:27.890
testing is a great use case
for cloud infrastructure.

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:31.190
It's a much more efficient
way to run tests.

00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:33.110
And so let's just talk
some numbers here.

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:36.170
So imagine your web
application, you only

00:13:36.170 --> 00:13:42.380
had 50 test cases or scripts
that you want to run.

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:45.290
I'm sure many of you have orders
of magnitude more than that,

00:13:45.290 --> 00:13:47.240
but let's start
small, so 50 things.

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:48.890
Let's say I want
to add some items

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:50.570
to a cart I want to checkout.

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:53.570
Let me search, let me sign up,
register, so on and so forth,

00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:57.069
like the core of
a web application.

00:13:57.069 --> 00:13:58.610
Now, let's imagine
for each of those,

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:00.890
you only want to test, on
average, three scenarios,

00:14:00.890 --> 00:14:03.650
so users with
different permissions,

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:07.220
or in different locations, or
different credit card numbers,

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:08.040
and so forth.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:09.650
So, let's say just three.

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:12.230
Now, you want to support
probably the four

00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:15.290
major browsers,
let's say, and maybe

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:18.050
the current version and
one previous version, so

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:19.760
eight browser versions.

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:22.150
And then, you have
three environments,

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:26.390
so you deploy to QA, and
then staging, and then prod.

00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:29.180
And you want to test
every time you deploy.

00:14:29.180 --> 00:14:31.370
And then you do
three releases a day,

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:35.270
which may sound like, oh my god,
that's an incredible velocity

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:36.320
to many of you.

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:40.190
And then to some others you'd
say, well, you mean just me,

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:42.625
not my whole team?

00:14:42.625 --> 00:14:44.750
But in this world where
you're deploying about once

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:49.280
every eight hours, you're
talking about nearly 11,000

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:51.470
tests being executed a day.

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:54.830
So this is real volume
generated from trying

00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:59.120
to get decent coverage
across only 50 tests.

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:00.800
Now, if you wanted to look at--

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:02.360
let's say, we ran--

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:05.210
we created tests that run pretty
quickly, seven minutes each

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:10.250
on average, you're talking
about nearly 1,300 compute hours

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:12.530
dedicated to
executing these tests.

00:15:12.530 --> 00:15:15.800
And you're talking about
a very, very simple test

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:17.720
suite and set of scenarios.

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:21.620
So this is real infrastructure
that we need to deal with.

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:23.810
Where it gets kind
of crazy is when

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:26.360
you think about the
concurrency that's

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:31.310
required in order to execute
those tests within target

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:31.850
windows.

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:35.420
So if you're doing a
release every eight hours,

00:15:35.420 --> 00:15:37.790
and you want to run those
tests that we talked

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:40.190
about in the prior
slide, then sure,

00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:45.020
if you had 54 nodes of compute,
executing tests in parallel,

00:15:45.020 --> 00:15:47.690
you could churn through
them in about eight hours,

00:15:47.690 --> 00:15:51.630
so before you do your
next release, not too bad.

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:54.770
Now, Joe doesn't like to
sit around for eight hours

00:15:54.770 --> 00:15:57.350
after he does a deployment
to wait to find out

00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:58.820
if the tests passed.

00:15:58.820 --> 00:16:01.590
In fact, he wouldn't
even tolerate an hour.

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:03.510
But if it was an
hour, he would--

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:06.690
we need about 420
nodes of compute

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:08.690
to be able to run all
those tests within an hour

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:11.390
after the deployment.

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:15.290
Now, what we expect, and
what I think many of you

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:19.222
expect when we get to that
vision of continuous delivery,

00:16:19.222 --> 00:16:20.680
is that we're
talking about minutes

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:23.960
to execute the entire test suite
because you want your engineers

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:26.090
to be able to get that
code in production,

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:27.710
and move on to the
next task, and not

00:16:27.710 --> 00:16:30.320
deal with a bunch
of work in progress.

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:33.890
And so here we're talking
about 3,600 nodes of compute

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:40.370
to execute those 50 simple
scripts for this application.

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:42.710
But the reason why
this is a perfect use

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:46.100
case for cloud computing
is because for how long

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:49.280
do you need those 3,600
nodes of compute, the line

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:50.480
in green here?

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:53.130
You need them for
about 10 minutes.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:56.780
And so why would you deal
with previsions, capacity,

00:16:56.780 --> 00:16:58.460
to execute those tests?

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:00.440
You want to burst,
run the tests,

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:01.880
and then make it disappear.

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:04.970
And the efficiency is
basically the entire chart

00:17:04.970 --> 00:17:07.349
because you're only paying
for the green spikes.

00:17:07.349 --> 00:17:09.210
You're not paying for the rest.

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:13.369
That's the most efficient model
for executing those tests.

00:17:13.369 --> 00:17:15.604
And we can show you
exactly how to do it.

00:17:15.604 --> 00:17:17.240
JOSEPH LUST: Thanks Dan.

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:19.190
So let's say you're
on the bandwagon,

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:22.205
you want to build your
cloud-centric, scalable

00:17:22.205 --> 00:17:25.290
Selenium-testing framework
Well, lucky for you,

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:28.869
Google Cloud Platform has
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:17:28.869 --> 00:17:30.410
Now, I need to give
you a disclaimer.

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:32.630
This won't let you
test everything.

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:34.670
Some browsers are limited
by their architectures

00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:37.580
or licenses, like
Internet Explorer, Safari

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:40.100
on Mac, iOS, and Android.

00:17:40.100 --> 00:17:44.810
But excluding those, you can
still run a very robust set up

00:17:44.810 --> 00:17:45.920
in the cloud.

00:17:45.920 --> 00:17:49.640
So for example, you could use
Selenium Docker project images

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:52.130
of Firefox and Chrome
and schedule them

00:17:52.130 --> 00:17:56.150
on your Google Kubernetes
Engine instances as pods.

00:17:56.150 --> 00:17:58.250
And Kubernetes is
now is automatically

00:17:58.250 --> 00:18:00.677
going to find a home for
each one of those pods.

00:18:00.677 --> 00:18:02.760
It's going to start stacking
them up in instances.

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:04.260
It's going to
efficiently pack them.

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:06.834
And if it can't find a
home, add more instances,

00:18:06.834 --> 00:18:08.250
and keep adding
instances until it

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:11.917
can run all those tests,
all 10,000, for example.

00:18:11.917 --> 00:18:13.500
But then when they're
done, it's going

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:15.916
to start getting rid of nodes,
scale all the way back down

00:18:15.916 --> 00:18:17.010
to zero.

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:19.890
This makes it highly efficient,
highly cost effective.

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:23.040
And it's actually
all very much no ops.

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:24.390
It just works.

00:18:24.390 --> 00:18:27.750
So at mabl, for example, we have
many such Kubernetes clusters.

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:29.700
But last year, we
set up one of them.

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:32.220
And to date, it's run about
12 million containers.

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:34.290
And it really hasn't
been touched in a year.

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:37.080
It automatically gets upgraded,
automatically maintained.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:39.930
It's a very, very robust set up.

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:41.354
We like to think
about Ron Popeil

00:18:41.354 --> 00:18:42.520
when we look at our cluster.

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:45.100
We just say, set
it and forget it.

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:46.980
Now again, using
these tools, you

00:18:46.980 --> 00:18:50.370
are easily able to take
your Selenium infrastructure

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:53.622
and just seamlessly
scale it in the cloud.

00:18:53.622 --> 00:18:55.240
DAN BELCHER: Great.

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:57.910
So what Joe will
show us next is how

00:18:57.910 --> 00:19:00.060
we've implemented that in mabl.

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:03.880
So imagine you didn't want to
provision your own Kubernetes

00:19:03.880 --> 00:19:06.280
architecture and deal with
the scheduling, and execution,

00:19:06.280 --> 00:19:08.920
and all that yourself,
if you were to use mabl,

00:19:08.920 --> 00:19:11.570
let's show them how that
would work in real time

00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:12.820
if we could bring up the demo.

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:13.570
JOSEPH LUST: Sure.

00:19:13.570 --> 00:19:15.220
So what we have
right here now is

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:19.302
an example of what we
call a test plan in mabl.

00:19:19.302 --> 00:19:21.010
In this case, we're
just going to kick it

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:23.020
off hitting the "Run" button.

00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:25.750
And what's just happened is
our pods, as you can see here

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:28.390
for Chrome and for
Firefox, have been sent off

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:30.670
to be scheduled on the
Kubernetes cluster.

00:19:30.670 --> 00:19:33.040
Actual browsers
are being spun up.

00:19:33.040 --> 00:19:35.900
And then we actually here
have our different tests.

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:38.131
So for example, we
have "find a shirt."

00:19:38.131 --> 00:19:40.630
And we can see in this case,
we're going to go to Nordstrom,

00:19:40.630 --> 00:19:42.171
we're going to look
for men's shirts,

00:19:42.171 --> 00:19:45.540
and we're going to assert that
we found the proper shirt.

00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:48.880
And what's going to happen
is that mabl's going

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:50.710
to make all those permutations.

00:19:50.710 --> 00:19:52.580
It's going to make two
tests, two browsers.

00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:55.012
So four containers
are going to spin up.

00:19:55.012 --> 00:19:56.470
And we can see them
all right here.

00:19:56.470 --> 00:19:59.050
Looks like they took off about
30 seconds ago all running

00:19:59.050 --> 00:20:01.970
in parallel.

00:20:01.970 --> 00:20:03.710
And what's going to
happen is mabl is

00:20:03.710 --> 00:20:05.876
making all those observations
we showed you earlier,

00:20:05.876 --> 00:20:08.300
taking all the screenshots,
learning everything she can

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:10.670
about this given
application under test,

00:20:10.670 --> 00:20:16.120
taking screenshots, and
letting us do all of this just

00:20:16.120 --> 00:20:18.940
in parallel, much more
scalable than anything

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:21.039
you generally would
be able to find when

00:20:21.039 --> 00:20:22.330
you're running your own set up.

00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:23.120
And here we are.

00:20:23.120 --> 00:20:25.960
We've already gone through--
we started finding pants.

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:30.280
And we are scaling
very well in the cloud.

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:33.280
We have the slides, Ken?

00:20:33.280 --> 00:20:34.860
DAN BELCHER: Thank you, Joe.

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:39.390
And so that's what we're talking
about when we talk about tests

00:20:39.390 --> 00:20:41.130
being the sort of--
automated testing

00:20:41.130 --> 00:20:44.460
being the ideal workload
for cloud computing.

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:49.210
We can kick off a test, fan
out, run that test in parallel,

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:51.480
turn the resources
off, and only pay

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:53.970
for the resources for that
very short period of time

00:20:53.970 --> 00:20:56.160
that we needed them
to execute the test.

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:59.040
You also noticed it's the
Anniversary sale at Nordstrom

00:20:59.040 --> 00:20:59.640
here.

00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:03.150
So, the third
principle, test results

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:04.500
need to be more insightful.

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:07.560
And so the core
there is as we're

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:12.420
executing tens of thousands
of tests over a given release

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:15.270
cycle, or quarter,
or year, we can't

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:19.410
be dealing with each test
run as a special snowflake.

00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:22.380
Joe would never go
into an application

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:24.540
and look at those
logs of the test

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:26.400
to make sure that it's
running effectively.

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:30.360
What he needs to know is,
did I break the product?

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:34.500
And otherwise, he's going
to be on to the next feature

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:36.120
that he's working on.

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:38.050
And so, how do we do that?

00:21:38.050 --> 00:21:41.820
Well, one principle for us with
making test results insightful

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:45.630
is we think, what would
mabl as a persona do?

00:21:45.630 --> 00:21:48.810
If mabl is a manual
tester, would mabl only

00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:52.110
look at the HTML of the page?

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:54.210
And is the HTML doing
what it's supposed to do?

00:21:54.210 --> 00:21:55.130
Probably not.

00:21:55.130 --> 00:21:57.360
Would mabl only look
at the screenshots

00:21:57.360 --> 00:21:59.430
and see if the screenshots
were appropriate?

00:21:59.430 --> 00:22:00.630
Probably not.

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:05.760
Mabl would want to look at a
variety of aspects of quality

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:09.390
of the application and
then decide whether it

00:22:09.390 --> 00:22:11.850
works or not based on those.

00:22:11.850 --> 00:22:13.860
And so what we've done
is built a pipeline that

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:17.790
can capture all these
different types of data,

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:20.490
we feed that data into
an analytics pipeline,

00:22:20.490 --> 00:22:24.750
and then we generate insights
based on either heuristics

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:28.890
or models that we've
implemented to detect

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:30.870
when the application's
not performing

00:22:30.870 --> 00:22:33.800
as we expected that it should.

00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:36.010
And so here is a good example.

00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:38.430
We have some very
simple insights.

00:22:38.430 --> 00:22:40.200
We can use basic heuristics.

00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:42.210
Many of you could
build this one where

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:45.810
we attempt to click on every
link in an application.

00:22:45.810 --> 00:22:47.210
And we have
heuristics that says,

00:22:47.210 --> 00:22:48.960
if there's a new broken
link, why don't we

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:52.260
tell Joe about it and say,
your last build actually

00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:54.910
broke some links, simple stuff.

00:22:54.910 --> 00:22:57.360
The next one, client-side
JavaScript errors.

00:22:57.360 --> 00:23:00.500
So, we'll capture client-side
JavaScript errors,

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:02.640
associate them with
each step in the test.

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:05.040
And the important
thing here is letting

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:08.010
people know when there's
a change versus the model

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:10.860
or state that we
have of the page.

00:23:10.860 --> 00:23:15.690
So many of you probably have
lots of client-side JavaScript

00:23:15.690 --> 00:23:18.900
errors when we load the
app that don't actually

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:20.340
hurt the end user.

00:23:20.340 --> 00:23:22.680
And so we're not going to
tell you again, and again,

00:23:22.680 --> 00:23:25.710
and again we saw again in this
test, wake up at 3 o'clock

00:23:25.710 --> 00:23:28.997
in the morning because
you have some errors.

00:23:28.997 --> 00:23:31.080
But what you might want
to know is when I just did

00:23:31.080 --> 00:23:32.930
a deployment that
I'm now on step four,

00:23:32.930 --> 00:23:34.470
there are new errors.

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:37.530
And so this gives you a way
of preventing technical debt

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:39.780
from accruing with these errors.

00:23:39.780 --> 00:23:43.230
And so these are two
heuristics-driven insights

00:23:43.230 --> 00:23:45.660
that this pipeline gives us.

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:50.080
Another one, model-driven
insights around anomalies.

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:52.710
So think of timing.

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:56.100
So I'm able as a manual
QA engineer would probably

00:23:56.100 --> 00:23:59.850
love to be able to detect
subtle changes in page load

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:02.100
time or test execution time.

00:24:02.100 --> 00:24:05.310
But that's a challenging
thing for us to do as humans,

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:08.310
running test after test
unless you have a stopwatch.

00:24:08.310 --> 00:24:11.520
But you're not in control
of all of your actions.

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:16.320
So what mabl will do is
captures timing information--

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:20.560
page load time, test execution
time, step time, and so forth--

00:24:20.560 --> 00:24:23.500
and then predicts
how long it will take

00:24:23.500 --> 00:24:25.000
the next time we run a test.

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:27.810
And if the actual observation is
different than the prediction,

00:24:27.810 --> 00:24:31.540
then we surface an insight.

00:24:31.540 --> 00:24:35.440
And of course, Google
Cloud, and machine learning

00:24:35.440 --> 00:24:37.840
play an important part in that.

00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:39.400
The feature that
people are perhaps

00:24:39.400 --> 00:24:42.160
most excited about
right now with us

00:24:42.160 --> 00:24:45.700
is detecting visual anomalies.

00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:48.370
And so our Nordstrom
colleagues in the audience

00:24:48.370 --> 00:24:49.960
will appreciate this one.

00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:51.520
When we search for those pants--

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:53.750
we started the test a
couple of days ago--

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:58.060
It was the preview of
the Anniversary sale.

00:24:58.060 --> 00:25:01.150
And then a couple of days
later, we ran the test,

00:25:01.150 --> 00:25:03.150
and the Anniversary
sale had been launched.

00:25:03.150 --> 00:25:04.690
And so what changed?

00:25:04.690 --> 00:25:06.160
You can barely see it.

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:09.970
But the menus is
at the top changed.

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:11.980
And that was the first
time mabl had ever

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:13.630
seen those menus changed.

00:25:13.630 --> 00:25:16.570
And so she flagged
it as an anomaly,

00:25:16.570 --> 00:25:18.220
as an anomalous change.

00:25:18.220 --> 00:25:21.430
Now what you can't really
see-- you could barely see here

00:25:21.430 --> 00:25:23.410
is when I run that
search for women's

00:25:23.410 --> 00:25:26.290
pants, the order
of the pants change

00:25:26.290 --> 00:25:28.270
every time you on the test.

00:25:28.270 --> 00:25:31.120
There's a random aspect
to how those load.

00:25:31.120 --> 00:25:33.460
And so mabl has
to be smart enough

00:25:33.460 --> 00:25:37.510
to detect expected changes
from unexpected changes.

00:25:37.510 --> 00:25:41.830
And so the model actually takes
these dynamic areas and says,

00:25:41.830 --> 00:25:43.570
while these are
dynamic regions, I'm

00:25:43.570 --> 00:25:46.840
not going to surface visual
anomalies in those regions

00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:48.600
because they change
too frequently,

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:50.350
whereas there are these
other regions that

00:25:50.350 --> 00:25:52.750
are predictably the same
every time I run the test.

00:25:52.750 --> 00:25:55.480
And if I see those
change, then I'll

00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:57.440
surface that as an insight.

00:25:57.440 --> 00:25:59.010
And so these are
the type of insights

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:02.190
that we're trying to drive
toward in this new era where

00:26:02.190 --> 00:26:05.270
we're giving you actionable,
relevant information

00:26:05.270 --> 00:26:07.350
so that then you
can dive in when

00:26:07.350 --> 00:26:08.670
there are potential issues.

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:10.720
But it takes a lot of work.

00:26:10.720 --> 00:26:12.570
Thankfully, we have
some infrastructure

00:26:12.570 --> 00:26:13.886
that we can rely on.

00:26:13.886 --> 00:26:15.840
JOSEPH LUST: Thanks Dan.

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:18.480
So as Dan points out, one of
these major limiting factors

00:26:18.480 --> 00:26:21.347
we have with tests in general is
that they make a lot of noise.

00:26:21.347 --> 00:26:23.930
As a developer, the last thing
you want is yet another report.

00:26:23.930 --> 00:26:26.310
Here is 6,000 things you
need to fix right now.

00:26:26.310 --> 00:26:28.980
So what comes out of this
process has to be insightful.

00:26:28.980 --> 00:26:33.600
But also like in mabl when
we're running on Scale and GCP,

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:35.760
we have to handle tons
and tons of inputs

00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:37.880
and figure out which ones
of those are insights.

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:39.838
And more importantly, we
have to tune them down

00:26:39.838 --> 00:26:42.600
to the exact insight for
that specific customer,

00:26:42.600 --> 00:26:44.490
whether it's insightful to them.

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:47.700
So we start, as you saw earlier,
with our Kubernetes Engine,

00:26:47.700 --> 00:26:50.670
which is churning through
thousands of web pages,

00:26:50.670 --> 00:26:53.350
making our continuous feed
of observations coming out.

00:26:53.350 --> 00:26:56.070
And then we feed it into
several different pipelines.

00:26:56.070 --> 00:26:59.430
The first pipeline is our
continuous prediction pipeline.

00:26:59.430 --> 00:27:03.160
Here, we harness Cloud Dataflow,
again, to work on the data,

00:27:03.160 --> 00:27:04.740
transform it is necessary.

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:06.574
And we feed it into Cloud ML.

00:27:06.574 --> 00:27:07.990
And in the Cloud
Machine Learning,

00:27:07.990 --> 00:27:10.770
we run our models against,
for example, those images

00:27:10.770 --> 00:27:14.040
or against timing, and we
decide, did this change?

00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:15.540
Is this a visual anomaly?

00:27:15.540 --> 00:27:18.270
Or did this get too
slow or too fast?

00:27:18.270 --> 00:27:20.820
And if that is the case,
then we immediately

00:27:20.820 --> 00:27:23.700
push via Pub/Sub
to Cloud Functions

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:25.150
so we can notify our user.

00:27:25.150 --> 00:27:27.960
We want to do that immediately
so it's a timely insight.

00:27:27.960 --> 00:27:30.600
And that, for example,
is sent out over Slack.

00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:32.550
So within about a second
or two from hitting

00:27:32.550 --> 00:27:35.670
that URL, observing, we popped
up a message in your Slack

00:27:35.670 --> 00:27:39.840
saying, we found something
is going way, way too slow.

00:27:39.840 --> 00:27:41.820
Yes or no, is this good?

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:43.290
But we can't just tell you that.

00:27:43.290 --> 00:27:45.956
We want to take that feedback
back into the machine

00:27:45.956 --> 00:27:47.580
because the machine
needs to understand

00:27:47.580 --> 00:27:48.889
what's important to you.

00:27:48.889 --> 00:27:50.430
So in this case,
Cloud Functions lets

00:27:50.430 --> 00:27:52.980
us have an endpoint that
takes that Slack feedback back

00:27:52.980 --> 00:27:55.360
in when you click on the,
yes, this is a good insight,

00:27:55.360 --> 00:27:57.660
or no, it's not, and
we feed that back

00:27:57.660 --> 00:28:00.570
into another pipeline, our
continuous training pipeline.

00:28:00.570 --> 00:28:02.220
We can do a little
bit of cleanup,

00:28:02.220 --> 00:28:03.840
for example, in Cloud Dataflow.

00:28:03.840 --> 00:28:06.280
And then again by
Cloud Machine Learning,

00:28:06.280 --> 00:28:09.870
we can update that model,
make it adapt to you

00:28:09.870 --> 00:28:11.010
and tune your insights.

00:28:11.010 --> 00:28:12.750
We won't give you an insight
that's not insightful to you

00:28:12.750 --> 00:28:13.740
in the future.

00:28:13.740 --> 00:28:15.480
We put this back in
the Cloud Datastore.

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:16.980
And that data store
is the reference

00:28:16.980 --> 00:28:20.399
of all of these models,
so we now have a circle.

00:28:20.399 --> 00:28:22.440
And we are continuously
training, and predicting,

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:23.770
and training.

00:28:23.770 --> 00:28:25.145
Another limiting
factor sometimes

00:28:25.145 --> 00:28:26.686
with creating these
sorts of insights

00:28:26.686 --> 00:28:28.410
is maybe you have
a new model, and we

00:28:28.410 --> 00:28:29.760
need to change that model.

00:28:29.760 --> 00:28:32.250
We need to maybe go back
through a 100 million pictures

00:28:32.250 --> 00:28:33.930
and retrain against all of them.

00:28:33.930 --> 00:28:36.240
That can be very prohibitive
from a scaling standpoint.

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:38.310
But using, for example,
Kubernetes Engine,

00:28:38.310 --> 00:28:41.430
we can take a brand
new Python ML script,

00:28:41.430 --> 00:28:45.100
we can, as we did at mabl
this week, spin 400 machines,

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:47.130
crush all of those
millions of images,

00:28:47.130 --> 00:28:49.230
update everybody's models.

00:28:49.230 --> 00:28:51.330
And now, we have been
able to push something out

00:28:51.330 --> 00:28:53.640
that would have been very
challenging previously back

00:28:53.640 --> 00:28:57.720
into our continuous prediction
and training pipeline.

00:28:57.720 --> 00:29:00.451
All this being
perfectly serverless,

00:29:00.451 --> 00:29:02.700
each one of these components
able to scale up and down

00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:05.354
independently with
NoOps and us, all

00:29:05.354 --> 00:29:07.020
through using these
different serverless

00:29:07.020 --> 00:29:09.650
technologies in Google Cloud.

00:29:09.650 --> 00:29:10.650
DAN BELCHER: Thanks Joe.

00:29:10.650 --> 00:29:14.110
So, if you wanted to build
this, you just do this, right?

00:29:14.110 --> 00:29:14.902
It's not that hard.

00:29:14.902 --> 00:29:15.901
JOSEPH LUST: It's great.

00:29:15.901 --> 00:29:18.220
DAN BELCHER: I mean, I
think the staggering thing

00:29:18.220 --> 00:29:21.760
for me is to think about where
we are as an industry today.

00:29:21.760 --> 00:29:25.220
And while this has taken
some work to build,

00:29:25.220 --> 00:29:30.280
for a team, PhDs making
this stuff happen,

00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:34.270
imagine what it would have
taken 10 years ago, where

00:29:34.270 --> 00:29:36.670
none of these services
existed, or at least were

00:29:36.670 --> 00:29:39.830
available to us publicly
outside of Google.

00:29:39.830 --> 00:29:45.250
And so it's actually making it
possible to solve some problems

00:29:45.250 --> 00:29:47.530
in a very elegant way
that weren't really

00:29:47.530 --> 00:29:49.240
possible to solve
before unless you

00:29:49.240 --> 00:29:52.380
were running at incredible
scale, Google scale

00:29:52.380 --> 00:29:54.050
or what have you.

00:29:54.050 --> 00:29:57.450
And so that was the
third principle,

00:29:57.450 --> 00:30:00.760
that test results need
to be more insightful.

00:30:00.760 --> 00:30:03.590
And then the final
one is testing then

00:30:03.590 --> 00:30:06.160
needs to be part of a
continuous delivery pipeline.

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:08.840
So QA can't be off to the side.

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:11.470
It needs to be fully automated
part of the pipeline.

00:30:11.470 --> 00:30:13.450
And what a pipeline
might look like is

00:30:13.450 --> 00:30:15.360
something like this, right Joe?

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:16.660
We're write some code.

00:30:16.660 --> 00:30:17.600
He'll commit it.

00:30:17.600 --> 00:30:20.320
We'll do a build,
execute some unit tests.

00:30:20.320 --> 00:30:24.460
Once that's ready to go, we'll
deploy to a QA environment.

00:30:24.460 --> 00:30:28.210
That deployment generates--
triggers some tests.

00:30:28.210 --> 00:30:31.090
Once those all pass,
then we promote

00:30:31.090 --> 00:30:34.420
that build to a
staging environment

00:30:34.420 --> 00:30:36.160
or deployed to a
staging environment.

00:30:36.160 --> 00:30:37.630
Then we run some
tests on staging.

00:30:37.630 --> 00:30:39.630
If those pass, we
deploy to prod,

00:30:39.630 --> 00:30:42.730
and then we'll do some
final smoke tests in prod.

00:30:42.730 --> 00:30:48.010
So let's see what that
looks like at scale.

00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:50.620
JOSEPH LUST: So as a
quick overview of the demo

00:30:50.620 --> 00:30:52.420
we're going to do,
we're going to show you

00:30:52.420 --> 00:30:55.120
how we can create
continuous testing

00:30:55.120 --> 00:30:58.000
and continuous deployment
workflows tied together to get

00:30:58.000 --> 00:31:00.750
us a testing feedback loop.

00:31:00.750 --> 00:31:04.300
In this case, we have a
CodeCommit, perhaps to GitHub.

00:31:04.300 --> 00:31:07.020
That can then trigger
a deployment trigger

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:09.550
in Cloud Build, which is
going to automatically

00:31:09.550 --> 00:31:11.320
go get our code from Git.

00:31:11.320 --> 00:31:14.350
It's going to build
it, deploy it, tell

00:31:14.350 --> 00:31:17.350
mabl there's a new deployment
available via its API.

00:31:17.350 --> 00:31:19.600
That will then come
back and test our app.

00:31:19.600 --> 00:31:22.510
And if it's successful, it
can call back to Cloud Build

00:31:22.510 --> 00:31:26.350
and keep propagating through
our deploy, test, deploy, test,

00:31:26.350 --> 00:31:27.940
deploy, test cycle.

00:31:27.940 --> 00:31:30.327
So, to show you what that
works like, if we could please

00:31:30.327 --> 00:31:31.160
get the demo laptop.

00:31:35.390 --> 00:31:36.765
The first thing
we need is an app

00:31:36.765 --> 00:31:38.223
because we have to
deploy something

00:31:38.223 --> 00:31:39.570
for our continuous deployment.

00:31:39.570 --> 00:31:43.880
So in this case, we have
calc.mabl.com, a very primitive

00:31:43.880 --> 00:31:46.880
calculator where we can
do things like addition.

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:52.680
Now, this demo is going to start
like all great code deployments

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:54.300
start, with a merge.

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:57.960
So we have our
live Lucky demo PR.

00:31:57.960 --> 00:31:59.955
Let's hope this works.

00:31:59.955 --> 00:32:01.410
Eh, do it.

00:32:01.410 --> 00:32:03.210
OK.

00:32:03.210 --> 00:32:05.360
So now we've just sent
this off into the ether.

00:32:05.360 --> 00:32:07.330
And we're going to be
triggering Cloud Build.

00:32:10.360 --> 00:32:12.610
And Cloud Build
should take over now.

00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:19.400
And it's discovered that we have
just started the deployment.

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:21.050
And this lets us do
a simple workflow.

00:32:21.050 --> 00:32:23.540
For example, we're going to
install our application's

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:24.590
dependencies.

00:32:24.590 --> 00:32:26.741
We're going to
build that web app.

00:32:26.741 --> 00:32:28.490
We're going to push
it out to Google Cloud

00:32:28.490 --> 00:32:30.270
Storage for static hosting.

00:32:30.270 --> 00:32:32.367
It's actually going to
give us a dynamic URL.

00:32:32.367 --> 00:32:34.700
And then we're going to call
mabl's deployment event API

00:32:34.700 --> 00:32:37.010
and feed in that dynamic
URL so we can actually

00:32:37.010 --> 00:32:40.640
run that test against
any different location.

00:32:40.640 --> 00:32:43.610
If we see there, it
looks like it's almost

00:32:43.610 --> 00:32:48.000
done building our web app.

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:51.480
But let's talk about what
it's actually going to do.

00:32:51.480 --> 00:32:54.960
So, we've got some more
tests here because, again,

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:57.450
for continuous testing, we're
going to need some tests.

00:32:57.450 --> 00:32:58.710
And in this case,
similar to before,

00:32:58.710 --> 00:33:00.835
we're going to run across
multiple browsers, Chrome

00:33:00.835 --> 00:33:01.710
and Firefox.

00:33:01.710 --> 00:33:03.660
And we're going to do
a simple mathematics

00:33:03.660 --> 00:33:05.660
test because we need to
make sure the math works

00:33:05.660 --> 00:33:08.110
inside that calculator we built.

00:33:08.110 --> 00:33:10.450
And so we have some
simple test steps here.

00:33:10.450 --> 00:33:15.609
For example, we want to make
sure that 3 plus 3 equals 6.

00:33:15.609 --> 00:33:16.900
That's a pretty good invariant.

00:33:16.900 --> 00:33:18.680
Hopefully it holds.

00:33:18.680 --> 00:33:20.050
But that's only one test.

00:33:20.050 --> 00:33:21.820
And a calculator
can do many things.

00:33:21.820 --> 00:33:24.463
So we actually have another
feature we're going to use.

00:33:24.463 --> 00:33:26.470
Oops, let's see.

00:33:26.470 --> 00:33:28.372
And that's called
parametrized testing

00:33:28.372 --> 00:33:29.830
because we don't
want to write tons

00:33:29.830 --> 00:33:31.392
and tons of different tests.

00:33:31.392 --> 00:33:33.350
So here we have something
we call a data table.

00:33:33.350 --> 00:33:35.110
So we have 50
different permutations.

00:33:35.110 --> 00:33:38.120
And now we can test things
like multiplication, division,

00:33:38.120 --> 00:33:39.790
subtraction, negative numbers.

00:33:39.790 --> 00:33:42.250
And now we're getting 100 tests.

00:33:42.250 --> 00:33:43.900
And all we had to do
is write one test.

00:33:43.900 --> 00:33:45.224
[LIGHT APPLAUSE]

00:33:45.224 --> 00:33:46.640
So given all that--
oh, thank you.

00:33:46.640 --> 00:33:47.290
Thank you.

00:33:47.290 --> 00:33:49.280
This feature's live, by the way.

00:33:49.280 --> 00:33:52.627
So given all of that, now
mabl has already started,

00:33:52.627 --> 00:33:53.960
and our servers are spinning up.

00:33:53.960 --> 00:33:55.890
Here they go off to the races.

00:33:55.890 --> 00:34:01.370
81, we should get to 100 here
shortly, little bit of a burst.

00:34:01.370 --> 00:34:02.934
But again, what's going on?

00:34:02.934 --> 00:34:04.850
All these separate
containers are spinning up.

00:34:04.850 --> 00:34:07.730
We have 100 instances pumping
in Google Cloud that just turned

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:09.949
on for Firefox, for Chrome.

00:34:09.949 --> 00:34:12.110
And they're running all
of our tests in parallel.

00:34:12.110 --> 00:34:14.090
And it looks like
they're done now.

00:34:14.090 --> 00:34:16.480
So that took under two
minutes, I believe.

00:34:16.480 --> 00:34:18.320
And in a classic
environment, that

00:34:18.320 --> 00:34:20.300
would have taken three hours.

00:34:20.300 --> 00:34:22.489
And all that was driven
this continuous testing,

00:34:22.489 --> 00:34:25.280
continues deployment, by
you making a single commit.

00:34:25.280 --> 00:34:26.620
DAN BELCHER: Woo!

00:34:26.620 --> 00:34:27.120
All right.

00:34:27.120 --> 00:34:28.996
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:28.996 --> 00:34:31.810
JOSEPH LUST: Slides.

00:34:31.810 --> 00:34:35.060
DAN BELCHER: So thank you, Joe.

00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:38.179
So we've talked through
the four principles

00:34:38.179 --> 00:34:40.219
that we've been
focused on that we

00:34:40.219 --> 00:34:44.300
think will underlie this new
era of intelligent testing.

00:34:44.300 --> 00:34:46.710
I know that if you
work in the QA space,

00:34:46.710 --> 00:34:48.572
there's been some
struggle lately keeping up

00:34:48.572 --> 00:34:49.780
with the pace of development.

00:34:49.780 --> 00:34:51.710
The good news is there is hope.

00:34:51.710 --> 00:34:54.260
We're going to, as an
industry, find a way

00:34:54.260 --> 00:34:57.350
to deliver tests that adapt
seamlessly to applications

00:34:57.350 --> 00:34:59.490
that change rapidly.

00:34:59.490 --> 00:35:03.740
We're going to run those tests
in the cloud at scale and speed

00:35:03.740 --> 00:35:07.520
that we haven't seen before,
as Joe's demo just showed us.

00:35:07.520 --> 00:35:11.600
We'll have insights based on
output that are actionable,

00:35:11.600 --> 00:35:14.510
so we don't have to deal
with the individual results

00:35:14.510 --> 00:35:15.200
of every test.

00:35:15.200 --> 00:35:18.769
And then also, we'll integrate
deeply into automated pipelines

00:35:18.769 --> 00:35:20.810
so that we don't have to
have manual intervention

00:35:20.810 --> 00:35:23.360
at every step in
a release process.

